13:14:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Don’t rely on trust alone; build a process that makes hard truths likely to surface. - Say explicitly that dissent is expected: “If you see a risk or think I’m wrong, I need you to tell me.” - Ask specific questions: “What am I missing?” “What would make this fail?” “What is the strongest case against this?” - Give defined authority and responsibility, so they have something concrete to own. - Respond calmly when they disagree—don’t punish, dismiss, or argue them into silence. - Use private check-ins or written feedback for sensitive issues. - Include independent reviews or escalation paths for high-stakes decisions. - During hiring, test for judgment and assertive communication with scenarios and references. That protects you against silence without assuming anyone is inherently cowardly.
